It does, but not as you might expect: on Julia 1.13.2 `%d` given a non-integer float rounds it instead of throwing. `%f`, `%e` and `%g` accept integers. `%%` prints a literal percent sign.

```julia
using Printf
@sprintf("%d", 3)          # → "3"
@sprintf("%d", 3.5)        # → "4"
@sprintf("%.1f", 3)        # → "3.0"
@sprintf("%5.1f%%", 12.345)  # → " 12.3%"
@sprintf("%s", :sym)       # → "sym"
@sprintf("%s|%s", 1, "a")  # → "1|a"
```

The format must be a literal string, because `@sprintf` parses it at macro-expansion time and checks the argument count. A non-literal format needs `Printf.Format` and `Printf.format` (see the earlier example). `@printf` writes to `stdout`; `@printf(io, ...)` writes to a stream.
